<!DOCTYPE node PUBLIC "-//freedesktop//DTD D-BUS Object Introspection 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/dbus/1.0/introspect.dtd">
<!--
  org.ruyaos.Play1 — install .play packages (docs/specs/05-play-packages.md) from a drive: list the
  candidate packages on mounted drives, look inside one, install it as ONE job, re-verify an
  installed one. Updates and feeds arrive later (T-241) and extend this file.
  Served by ruya-play on the system bus at /org/ruyaos/Play1 (RUY-829; services/ruya-play,
  tests/contract.rs holds it to this file). The shell's mock backend, shell/src/play, follows the
  same contract off Linux. Verify (RUY-834) re-hashes the deployed
  files of an installed app against the play.files the commit stored in them, as a job.
  Draft, internal: no element carries org.ruyaos.Stability, so nothing here is a developer
  surface (ADR-022).
  Contract between shell (Qt/QML) and services (Rust); see docs/specs/05-play-packages.md §7.3.

  Method statuses (stable API once released; the names of Store1 are reused where the meaning is
  the same):
    "ok"                success (Install/Verify: the job was queued, its result arrives with
                        JobFinished)
    "unknown-source"    the path is not one of the candidates ListSources lists (Inspect, Install):
                        a drive that was unplugged, or a path that was never offered
    "unknown-app"       Verify of an app that is not installed from a .play
    "unknown-job"       Cancel of a job the service does not know
    "too-late"          Cancel of a job that passed the point of no return (phase "committing")
    "busy"              a job for that package or app is already queued or running
    "forbidden"         Install without options "admin_approved" = true. Sideloading needs an admin
                        or an admin's PIN (05 §18 item 4); the shell checks the PIN through
                        Profiles1, as for the Store (PINs never leave the shell's path to
                        Profiles1). Trust boundary, enforced by ruya-play: the option counts only
                        when the caller's uid (GetConnectionUnixUser) is ruya-shell or 0; the bus
                        policy lets only ruya-shell and root call Play1 and it is never on
                        ruya-link's allow-list; and the service applies the tier ceilings,
                        developer mode and the refusal of unverified + high risk itself, whatever
                        the option says
    "invalid"           malformed argument (a relative path, an empty id, a bad option type)
    "failed"            internal error

  JobFinished statuses (one job covers a package from the first byte read to "installed"):
    "ok"                done (for Verify: every file matches play.files)
    "invalid-package"   a rule of §4 was broken or a hash did not match (Inspect shows the problems;
                        for Verify: the installed payload no longer matches play.files)
    "bad-signature"     the package was changed after it was signed (05 §9.2: never downgraded to
                        unverified)
    "different-developer" the id is installed (or its saves are kept) under another key (05 §9.2, key
                        continuity)
    "needs-developer-mode" the package is unverified and developer mode is off (05 §7.2)
    "revoked"           the package, its key or its content is on the block list (05 §9.2)
    "unsupported"       format.too_new, or an architecture other than x86_64
    "disk-full"         not enough space
    "cancelled"         Cancel reached the job in time
    "failed"            internal error

  Jobs are named by an opaque string returned from Install/Verify. The shell sees ONE job from
  import to installed. The Flatpak transaction itself runs in ruya-store's one queue (05 §18 item 2)
  inside the "installing" phase; the shell never sees it as a second job.
  JobProgress phases, in this order (a phase may be skipped, never repeated): "staging" (copy into
  the staging directory, hashing as it goes), "verifying" (signature, key continuity, file table),
  "vetting-art" (decode and re-encode art and video), "committing" (the root part builds the OSTree
  commit), "installing" (the Store's Flatpak transaction). A Verify job only reports "verifying".
  percent is 0-100 for the whole job and never decreases.

  Every list is a plain array and every record an a{sv} dictionary, so fields can be added later
  without breaking clients (unknown keys are ignored). Optional keys are absent when unknown: a
  client never shows "Unknown" for them.

  Source record (ListSources), keys:
    drive           s    the drive's id (Devices1 volume id)
    drive_label     s    the drive's name for display
    path            s    absolute path of the package: a folder or a .play.zip; this is what Inspect
                         and Install take
    name            s    the file or folder name (NOT read from the package: nothing was opened)
    kind            s    "folder" | "zip"
    size            t    bytes on disk (a folder: the sum of its files, at most as far as it was
                         cheap to know; 0 = unknown)

  Inspect record (a{sv}), keys. Everything comes from the unprivileged helper's result (05 §7.3
  step 2); no payload byte was copied.
    id              s    the manifest id, e.g. "com.example.starhopper" (the installed app is
                         "play." + id)
    name            s    display name in the player's language, else English
    summary         s    one line, same rule
    version         s    version text
    build           u    the integer that orders versions
    developer       s    developer name
    genres          as   raw genre keys
    players         s    "1", "1-2", "2-4", ...
    controller      s    "full" | "partial" | "none"
    age_label       s    the age label the package declares ("all" | "7+" | "12+" | "16+" | "18+"); absent
                         when it declares none (not "all")
    size            t    bytes the installed package will take
    requirement     s    "ok" | "unsupported" (format too new or another architecture)
    verdict         s    what Install would finish with if an admin started it now: "ok" or a
                         JobFinished status ("invalid-package", "bad-signature",
                         "different-developer", "needs-developer-mode", "revoked", "unsupported").
                         A hint for the page; Install repeats every check.
    tier            s    "official" | "developer" | "unverified"
    signer_key_id   s    32 hex characters (05 §9.2); absent for "unverified" without a signature
    signer_name     s    the name in the signature; absent as above
    key_state       s    "new" (this id was never installed) | "same" (installed under this key) |
                         "different" (installed or kept under another key). Absent for an
                         unsigned package
    permissions     a(sss) (key, words, risk) in the order of 05 §8; key = the vocabulary key with its
                         value ("network", "input:controllers+keyboard-mouse", "devel"); words = the
                         plain-language line in English (the shell may translate by key); risk =
                         "low" | "medium" | "high"
    gate            s    what the tier's ceiling (05 §8) makes of the requests: "allowed" |
                         "admin-pin" (official, high risk) | "admin-pin-warning" (developer, high
                         risk: the PIN after a warning). Absent when the package did not pass the
                         trust checks (a request above the ceiling is a problem, ceiling.refused).
                         Install needs admin_approved for every package whatever the gate; the
                         shell uses the gate to ask for the admin's PIN (RUY-102)
    permissions_added   a(sss) for an update (the id is installed): the requests this build adds to
                         the installed one's, as in permissions. Absent when the id is not
                         installed or the installed app's requests cannot be read
    permissions_removed a(sss) the requests the installed build makes and this one drops; absent
                         as above
    adds_high_risk  b    true when permissions_added has a high-risk request (05 §8: such an
                         update needs the admin's PIN); absent as above
    art             a{ss} role ("icon", "hero", "card", "cover", "logo", ...) -> path of the VETTED
                         file in staging (05 §5); absent roles were not supplied
    screenshots     as   paths of vetted screenshots (thumbnails not listed)
    revoked         b    true when the package is on the block list
    installed_build u    the build installed now; absent when the id is not installed
    problems        a(sss) (code, path, message) of 05 §4.5 and ruya-play-art (art.*, video.*).
                         The shell maps the code to its own translated text and never shows
                         message. Empty when the package is clean; a package with problems has
                         verdict "invalid-package" (or "unsupported" for format.too_new)

  Nothing is opened, run or mounted by ListSources (05 §9.1: no autorun). A package is only read
  after the player picks it (Inspect).
-->
<node>
  <interface name="org.ruyaos.Play1">

    <!-- Candidate packages on mounted removable drives: folders with a play.json and *.play.zip
         files, paths and names only. Never opens one. Drives without candidates are absent. -->
    <method name="ListSources">
      <arg name="sources" type="aa{sv}" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!-- Look inside one candidate (05 §7.3 step 2). status "ok" returns the record; any other
         status returns an empty dictionary. A package that breaks a rule is still "ok": its problems
         are in the record. -->
    <method name="Inspect">
      <arg name="path" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="info" type="a{sv}" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!--
      Queue the installation of a candidate. job is "" unless status is "ok".
      options (a{sv}, unknown keys are ignored):
        admin_approved  b   the shell vouches that an admin is signed in or gave the PIN. Required
                            (without it: "forbidden").
    -->
    <method name="Install">
      <arg name="path" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="options" type="a{sv}" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="job" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!-- Re-hash an installed package against its play.files (05 §7.3). app_id is the Flatpak
         application id, "play." + the manifest id (what Library1 and Session1 call it). job is ""
         unless status is "ok". -->
    <method name="Verify">
      <arg name="app_id" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="job" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!-- Stop a queued or running job. status "ok" means the job will finish with "cancelled". -->
    <method name="Cancel">
      <arg name="job" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!-- Developer mode (05 7.2): an admin's setting, off by default, that lets unsigned packages
         install. GetDeveloperMode reads it; SetDeveloperMode changes it. options (a{sv}):
           admin_approved  b   as for Install; without it status is "forbidden".
         status: "ok", "forbidden", "failed". DeveloperModeChanged follows every change. -->
    <method name="GetDeveloperMode">
      <arg name="on" type="b" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <method name="SetDeveloperMode">
      <arg name="on" type="b" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="options" type="a{sv}" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>
    <signal name="DeveloperModeChanged">
      <arg name="on" type="b"/>
    </signal>

    <!--
      The service's job table: the jobs queued or running and the last finished ones (the 20 most
      recent), oldest first. The shell calls it when it (re)connects and replaces what it shows by
      the answer (same rules as Store1.ListJobs). One dictionary per job:
        id       s   the job name returned by Install/Verify
        kind     s   "install" | "verify"
        path     s   install: the package path; verify: ""
        app      s   the Flatpak id play.<id>, the key Verify takes (install: once the manifest
                     is read, else "")
        state    s   "queued" | "running" | "finished"
        status   s   "" unless state is "finished", then the JobFinished status
        reason   s   "" or "interrupted" (the service died while the job ran; status "failed")
        percent  u   0-100 (100 for an "ok" job, 0 for a queued one)
        phase    s   as JobProgress, "" while queued
    -->
    <method name="ListJobs">
      <arg name="jobs" type="aa{sv}" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!-- phase: see the header; percent 0-100, never decreasing within a job. -->
    <signal name="JobProgress">
      <arg name="job" type="s"/>
      <arg name="percent" type="u"/>
      <arg name="phase" type="s"/>
    </signal>

    <!-- Always sent once per job. For an "ok" install the app is in the system installation when
         this is sent; ruya-library's watcher of the installation lists it (the Store's
         InstalledChanged is not sent for it). -->
    <signal name="JobFinished">
      <arg name="job" type="s"/>
      <arg name="status" type="s"/>
    </signal>

    <!-- A drive was mounted or unmounted, or a candidate appeared or went: call ListSources again. -->
    <signal name="SourcesChanged"/>

  </interface>
</node>
